KenshiLib_Examples_deps commit e75769b never provides a reconstructed
definition of `CraftingItem` anywhere - it's only ever forward-declared, in
kenshi/Building/CraftingBuilding.h. That header also declares a
`std::deque<CraftingItem, std::allocator<CraftingItem> > crafting;` member,
which needs a *complete* type to instantiate under MSVC10's STL. A bare
forward declaration isn't enough, so any translation unit that pulls this
header in (KenshiCoop's plugin does, via the Building/GameWorld chain) hits
MSVC error C2027 'CraftingItem': use of undefined type.

Fix: give CraftingItem a minimal empty-class definition in place of the
forward declaration. Nothing in KenshiCoop touches CraftingItem's members
(grep the plugin source for "CraftingItem" - no hits), so a stand-in is
sufficient; this only exists to satisfy the compiler, not to model the real
type.

Apply from the KenshiCoop repo root after fetching+pinning KenshiLib_deps
(see README.md in this folder for the fetch/checkout steps):

    git apply third_party/KenshiLib_patches/0002-add-craftingitem-stub-definition.patch

--- a/third_party/KenshiLib_deps/KenshiLib/Include/kenshi/Building/CraftingBuilding.h
+++ b/third_party/KenshiLib_deps/KenshiLib/Include/kenshi/Building/CraftingBuilding.h
@@ -7,7 +7,13 @@
 class InventoryLayout;
 class Faction;
 class DatapanelGUI;
-class CraftingItem;
+
+// CraftingItem has no reconstructed definition anywhere in this vendor
+// headers snapshot (KenshiLib_Examples_deps), only ever forward-declared here.
+// A forward declaration isn't a complete type, but std::deque<CraftingItem>
+// below needs one to instantiate under MSVC10's STL. Nothing in this project
+// touches CraftingItem's members, so a minimal stand-in is sufficient.
+class CraftingItem {};

 // TODO move?
 class GameDataGroup
